so I recently completed a review of re0  was uploaded and haha was it a literal  minefield of comments and opinions I  have not had a comment section this  heated since I've done a video sword art  online which is always guaranteed to set  up a bombshell on one side yet the usual  group of hype anime haters finding it  difficult to even fathom how such a show  got so popular on the other side were  the die-hard fans who deflected any  criticism thrown against it and  somewhere lost in the middle were the  people who are just like yeah it was  right but the one thing I constantly  noticed on the side that were defending  re0 was the continuous reference to the  source material to counter any criticism  that was made this guy dismisses the  point of the larger story every problem  we had gets answered in arc for anyway  are you an absolute idiot gigger you're  criticizing the narrative when it's just  the prologue the prologue which after  hearing well that's I could only think  one thing here we [ __ ] go again  see ignoring the fact that a prologue  that last 25 episodes already has flaws  in its narrative structure as longer  stories has been able to set up a  narrative in the much shorter time and  that longer prologue that have existed  before like berserk are essentially  self-contained stories with their own  narrative and that there is absolutely  no indication at all that this is just  the prologue please don't make this an  argument about where the Reiser is a  good prologue or not I did like the show  guys I'm just trying to make a larger  point here the bigger issue here is that  people are using the source material to  excuse the criticisms of an adaptation  and honestly this isn't the first time  this has happened and by god won't it be  the last either we see this a lot with  adaptations that have yet to be complete  or source material that is just really  hard to adapt like oh you guessed it the  faith franchise the granddaddy of this  entire debate see whenever I've seen  someone criticize either one of the Fate  adaptations it's almost guaranteed to  trigger a plethora of fans of the visual  novels it was based on either we were  watching it wrong or the criticisms we  had were never really true because they  were addressed in the visual novels and  we should have really read the visual  novel before watching the anime because  that's the only real way to prop the  experience tips and while fate is  definitely the easiest example I can  think of this way of thinking is  scattered across many different anime  franchises the peculiar thing is that  anime is any place I've really seen a  source material brought up as an actual  defense of the  patience as I definitely don't see this  as much in other mediums for example we  don't see the Watchmen comics used to  cover up any faults as movie had or any  other film adaptations for that matter  help we don't even see anime used as an  excuse for bad anime adaptations because  that's exactly how they're seen just a  bad adaptation outside of anime sees  more widely acknowledged that an  adaptation is separate from the source  material and it's judged independently  we see the words based on and we  immediately accept that whether this be  a good adaptation or bad adaptation it  should be judged based on its own merits  and that's the way it should be every  medium has different strengths and  weaknesses and you'd be dumb to think  that a complete one-on-one adaptation  would work for every story in every  medium what's important in my opinion is  that the adaptation contains the essence  of the source material Lord of the Rings  is a great example of this as for the  critical and commercial success that it  was  huge chunks of the original books were  cut out honestly leaving the films as an  incomplete picture of Tolkien's entire  universe yet it didn't matter because it  was a standalone piece in a medium that  yours in regardless of what it was based  on for this to happen the source  material must be viewed as a completely  separate piece of work  in other words irrelevant this is not to  say that we ignore the existence of the  source material at all but when judging  an adaptation it is important that we  look at both pieces of work individually  and not as a whole so why does it seem  like anime is treated differently to  other mediums  well anime is also a unique medium in  the sense as it's more used to seeing  one-for-one adaptations since with the  history of anime production which is  adapting manga stories the manga it was  based on is almost used as complete  storyboard an anime has evolved over the  years with this principle because of  this the majority of modern anime don't  even adapt the source material all the  way to the end due to the anime  production cycle these days unlike with  American TV shows which normally get  some kind of ending when the network  cuts it off or movies which only really  adapts things that are already complete  the amount of anime which only gets one  or two seasons with open endings never  to be seen again  is vast we're forced to judge the  adaptations we have because well we have  no guarantee that we'll ever see more of  it but this does beg the question of  whether these kinds of adaptation are  truly standalone or more of a  complementary piece of the source  material and extension of the original  body of work see  as I mentioned before with movies and  their source material being judged  entirely independently a lot of the  times anime adaptations can just be a  part of a bigger whole as I mentioned  with face they night and 80-plus our  visual novel with multiple routes and a  nonlinear narrative it is very difficult  my own impossible to create a faithful  adaptation where you get the same  experience as you do reading the visual  novels the same can be said with  incomplete adaptations like three zero  so far vote to a less complicated extent  so with all this in mind should we think  of anime is just an extension of the  original body of work no for me to think  that would be to undersell anime as a  medium and their subsequent adaptations  there is so much more to judge when it  comes to an anime adaptation you have  animation art voice acting music  cinematography and so many more elements  that blend into a product that warrants  it to be judged by its own merits we  have good adaptations and bad  adaptations and even if we don't get the  complete story it's still the animes job  to give you some sense of closure at the  end of the season even if it's not the  true ending this is why anything like  the original berserk anime are so  detested because they left us right in  the middle of a [ __ ] arc  this doesn't even take into account  anime original endings because like them  or not they are their own separate thing  and have been shown that they can work  you know at least once going back to the  Lord of the Rings example you don't need  a complete adaptation for it to work you  just need an adaptation that works and  the medium is being presented on and  it's not the source materials job to  cover up any faulty adaptation has is  the adaptations job to make sure it  works in the first place for example if  this season of re0 is just the prologue  where is the indication in the  adaptation that this indeed is just the  prologue plus if we can properly judge  something until the entire story is over  that would mean that like 90% of anime  would-be unreviewable I mean how can we  probably judge unless it's complete yet  guys there's still like 400 volumes of  manga left to adapt you wouldn't  possibly know if it's good until you've  seen absolutely all of it in the end  what this really is about is fans  wanting others to share their experience  they had with a certain franchise and  there's nothing inherently wrong with  that I've been on both sides of the  fence with this one both as an anime  only watch of watching a so-called awful  adaptation and the fan trying to defend  a franchise because an adaptation did  not live to what I wanted it to be  inevitably we will always be more  fected with the medium we first  experienced asourian and when someone  can't see the entire picture of what you  experience because of a shoddy or  incomplete adaptation we feel the need  to defend the experience we had so can  we all just for a moment take a step  back and see that adaptations are not  the source material  different adaptations can have different  experiences that are judged entirely  independently so someone criticizes the  adaptation they are criticizing your  experience or the thing as a whole it's  just simply what they saw or maybe they  just have a shitty opinion like me  [Music]
 

